.TH OTPME-MOUNT 1 "2026-10-07" "OTPme 0.3.0a280" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-mount \- mount and unmount OTPme file shares
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-mount
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I share
.RI [ mountpoint ]
.br
.B otpme-mount \-a
.br
.B otpme-mount \-u
.I share
.br
.B otpme-mount \-a \-u
.br
.B otpme-mount \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-mount
mounts a file share of your realm with FUSE.
The share lives on the nodes, the mount runs on your computer, and every request goes to a node over the network.
See
.BR otpme\-share (1)
for the shares themselves.
.PP
You must be logged in:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool login \fIuser\fR
.RE
.PP
A share is mounted with your token, so the mount shows what that token may see.
.SS Which share
A share has an ID, and that is what this command takes:
.PP
.RS
.I <realm>/<site>/<share>
.RE
.PP
The realm is part of it because two realms may have a site of the same name.
Leave it out and write \fI<site>/<share>\fR; then it is a share of your own realm.
.SS Where it is mounted
Give a mountpoint and the share is mounted there.
.PP
Leave it out and \fBotpme\-agent\fR mounts the share at the usual place:
.PP
.RS
.I /otpme/<user>/<realm>/<site>/<share>
.RE
.PP
The first part is the mount root (\fBMOUNT_ROOT_DIR\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5)).
The directories are made as needed and belong to you.
.PP
This is also where the shares of a user are mounted at login.
A mount made by hand with a mountpoint of your own is yours alone: the agent does not know about it, so it is not unmounted with the others.
.SS Which node
By default the nodes of the share are asked for, and one of them is picked at random.
That spreads the load.
When a node cannot be reached the next one is tried.
.PP
With
.B \-\-nodes
you say which nodes to use instead.
.SS A node that goes away
A share is a network filesystem, and a node can go away while the share is mounted.
Then every request fails right away, so a program sees an error instead of hanging.
.PP
With
.B \-\-hard
a write waits instead.
It waits until a node answers again, however long that takes, which keeps a program from losing data it has already handed over.
Everything else still fails right away.
.SS Encrypted shares
The data of an encrypted share is encrypted on your computer, with a key the share keeps for your token.
The key never reaches the node in clear.
.PP
Two things follow from that.
The key is handed to you when the share is mounted and is decrypted with the encrypt key of your user, so with \fByubikey_piv\fR the token has to be plugged in.
And the key is held against the share before a single request is served: the share keeps a value that only its own key reads.
A mount with another key is turned away, instead of writing data that nobody can read afterwards.
.PP
A share made before that value existed gets one from the first mount whose key reads one of the names in it.
Where no name reads, the mount is turned away as well: either the key is the wrong one, or the share holds data that was written with a wrong one.
Files in the share root that this filesystem did not write \(em a \fBlost+found\fR, something the admin put there \(em say nothing either way and are left out of it.
.PP
.B \-\-master\-password\-mount
asks for the master password of the share instead and makes the key from it.
Only a master password token may do this (see
.BR otpme\-share (1)).
It is the way in when no token of yours holds a key for the share.
.PP
.B \-\-add\-share\-key
stores the key that was made from the master password for your own token, so later mounts need no master password.
It needs \fB\-\-master\-password\-mount\fR, and therefore a token that may use the master password of the share.
.PP
It also has to run in the login session of the system user \fBroot\fR.
That is the session and not the command: the user is read from the agent, so a \fBsudo\fR in front of this command does not make one.
Log in as root and run it there:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-tool login \fIuser\fR
.br
.B otpme\-mount \-\-master\-password\-mount \-\-add\-share\-key \fIshare mountpoint\fR
.RE
.PP
This is also how a share is taken over whose data is older than its share object, see \fB\-\-no\-key\-gen\fR in
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Mount all shares you may mount, each at the usual place.
Not for a mountpoint of your own, and not together with \fB\-\-foreground\fR, \fB\-\-master\-password\-mount\fR or \fB\-\-add\-share\-key\fR.
.TP
.B \-u
Unmount the share.
With \fB\-a\fR it unmounts all of them.
The agent does the unmounting, so this reaches the mounts it made \(em the ones at the usual place.
A mount with a mountpoint of your own is not one of them; unmount that one with
.BR fusermount (1).
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the shares you may mount.
.TP
.B \-\-nodes \fInode1,node2\fR
Use these nodes instead of the ones the share names.
.TP
.B \-\-hard
Let a write wait while no node can be reached, instead of failing.
See \fBA node that goes away\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypted
Say that the share is encrypted.
Only needed when a mountpoint and \fB\-\-nodes\fR are both given: then nothing is asked of the management daemon, and this is the only way it is known.
.TP
.B \-\-master\-password\-mount
Make the key of an encrypted share from its master password.
You are asked for the password.
.TP
.B \-\-add\-share\-key
Store the key made from the master password for your own token.
Needs \fB\-\-master\-password\-mount\fR, and the login session of the system user \fBroot\fR.
See \fBEncrypted shares\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Keep the mount in this process instead of leaving it behind in one of its own.
The command ends when the share is unmounted.
Useful for looking at what a mount does.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-mount berlin/projects
Mount the share \fIprojects\fR of site \fIberlin\fR at \fI/otpme/<user>/<realm>/berlin/projects\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-mount example.org/berlin/projects /mnt/projects
Mount it at \fI/mnt/projects\fR, with the realm written out.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-a
Mount every share you may mount.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-u berlin/projects
Unmount that share.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-a \-u
Unmount all shares.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-\-list
Which shares are there for you.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-\-hard berlin/vmail /mnt/vmail
Mount with writes that wait for a node instead of failing.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-\-master\-password\-mount berlin/secure /mnt/secure
Mount an encrypted share with its master password.
.TP
.B otpme-mount \-\-master\-password\-mount \-\-add\-share\-key berlin/secure /mnt/secure
The same, and keep the key for your own token, so the next mount needs no master password.
Runs in the login session of root.
.SH NOTES
An unencrypted share connects in the background and answers with \fBENOENT\fR until the connection stands.
So a program that looks at the mountpoint in the first moment is told the path is not there, instead of waiting for it.
An encrypted share waits instead: getting its key may ask for a password, and that needs a command that waits for the answer.
.PP
A share can drop its connection when nothing uses it for a while, and with it the share key (\fBmount_timeout\fR, see
.BR otpme\-share (1)).
The next request connects again.
For an encrypted share that means the key is asked for again, so a token that has to be plugged in has to be plugged in again.
.PP
A node asks once a second whether your token may still use the share, and stops serving the mount when it may not.
Taking a permission away also tells the hosts to unmount the share, see \fB\-\-share\-notify\fR in
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.PP
After a mount that went to the background, the readahead of the mountpoint is set with
.B sudo -n setreadahead
.IR mountpoint .
Without that sudo rule the mount still works, only slower on large reads.
